{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And one of them opened his sack</b> — One of them opened his sack at the lodging place to give fodder from it to his donkey, and the others did not open their sacks until they were with their father, as it says, \"and it came to pass, as they were emptying,\" for perhaps the others would take straw for their donkeys, since they would not eat all their load on the road, and this one's donkey was not strong and needed to be fed, and he found his money at the mouth of his sack. And Rashi wrote that אַמְתַּחַת is the sack. And Onkelos translated \"טוּעְנָא\" (load), and what seems reasonable on its own is that each one had a large sack and small sacks to balance out their load, and the entire load is called אַמְתַּחַת, and it happened to one of them that he found his money at the mouth of that particular sack which he opened, and the like did not happen to the others until they were emptying all of them. And what seems closest to me is that an אַמְתַּחַת is a large sack that has two sides, called in the language of the Talmud (Ketubot 101a) \"מַטְרָאתָא,\" and at the mouth of one of the sides the money was placed for all of them, and it happened to one of them that he opened the side in which the money was and not to the others, and it is called אַמְתַּחַת because it extends (נמתח) to the sides."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 42, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:44:10.459773828Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}